// Typecheck configuration for the test suite.
//
// ─── Why this file exists ──────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// tsconfig.json is the PRODUCTION build (`rootDir: src`, `include:
// ["src/**/*"]`), so `tsc --noEmit` compiled 58 files from src/ and exactly
// ZERO from tests/. Measured, not assumed:
//
//     npx tsc --noEmit --listFiles | grep -c server.tests   →  0
//     npx tsc --noEmit --listFiles | grep -c server.src     →  58
//
// That gap is not cosmetic, and vitest does not cover it either. vitest
// transforms a test file with Vite's SSR pipeline, which rewrites
// `import { foo } from "../src/pvp.js"` into a property read on the module
// namespace — so an export that no longer exists arrives as `undefined`
// instead of failing to link, and the test only fails if something happens to
// CALL it. Both halves measured on this repo's own src/pvp.ts:
//
//   vite-node (the transform vitest uses)
//       import { startBattle, thisExportDoesNotExist } from ".../src/pvp.ts"
//       → startBattle: function, thisExportDoesNotExist: undefined,
//         module linked with NO error
//   real Node ESM (tsx), same file
//       → SyntaxError: The requested module does not provide an export named
//         'thisExportDoesNotExist'
//
// So "tsc is clean and the suite is green" did NOT prove that everything the
// tests import still exists — which is exactly the class of defect a rebuild
// of src/pvp.ts risks. This config closes the type half.
// tests/pvpExportSurface.test.ts closes the runtime half inside the suite
// itself, because a test run must not depend on somebody having run tsc.
//
// `npm run typecheck` runs the production config AND this one.
// This config never emits.
//
// ─── The exclusions are a ratchet, not a shrug ─────────────────────────
//
// Five test files carry pre-existing type errors (12 in total). They are
// excluded BY NAME so that this config is green today and any NEW type error
// in any of the other 18 test files — including five of the six PvP spec
// files — fails the typecheck immediately. Verbatim, so removing an
// exclusion is a known, bounded job rather than an exploration:
//
//   tests/pvpBadgeTierDrift.test.ts(106,40) TS2367 comparison between
//       'PvpBadgeTierId' and '"master"' has no overlap
//   tests/pvpBot.test.ts(646,44)  TS2345 'string' not assignable to 'ID'
//   tests/pvpBot.test.ts(917,20)  TS2345 'BotPokemon' not assignable to
//       '{ evs: Record<string, number> }' (GameEvs lacks an index signature)
//   tests/pvpBot.test.ts(921,20)  TS2345 as above
//   tests/pvpLadderSettle.test.ts(609,48)  TS2493 tuple '[]' has no index 0
//   tests/pvpLadderSettle.test.ts(1073,41) TS2493 as above
//   tests/saveGainGuard.test.ts(1199,12 / 1223,12 / 1244,12) TS2571 object
//       is of type 'unknown'
//   tests/savesRoute.test.ts(367,12 / 368,12 / 369,12) TS18046 'json' is of
//       type 'unknown'
//
// None of the five is editable from here: pvpBot.test.ts is one of the six PvP
// spec files, and saveGainGuard.test.ts / savesRoute.test.ts belong to the
// separate unshipped save-guard workstream. pvpBadgeTierDrift.test.ts and
// pvpLadderSettle.test.ts belong to the ladder/badge workstream. Each is a
// test-local annotation problem, not a production-type problem.
{
  "extends": "./tsconfig.json",
  "compilerOptions": {
    // The base sets rootDir to ./src, which cannot contain tests/. The repo
    // root is used rather than `server/` because tests/pvpBattleView.test.ts
    // deliberately imports the CLIENT decoder (game/src/state/pvpBattleView.ts)
    // to test both halves of the protocol against one another; with
    // `rootDir: "."` that is TS6059 "not under rootDir".
    "rootDir": "..",
    "noEmit": true,
    "types": ["node"]
  },
  "include": ["src/**/*", "tests/**/*"],
  "exclude": [
    "node_modules",
    "dist",
    "tests/pvpBadgeTierDrift.test.ts",
    "tests/pvpBot.test.ts",
    "tests/pvpLadderSettle.test.ts",
    "tests/saveGainGuard.test.ts",
    "tests/savesRoute.test.ts"
  ]
}
